    "justification": "This benchmark-only lemma supplies the constructive half of the field-grade minimax frontier.",
    "gap": "Existing results do not give a total polynomial-time honest nuclear ball with every expected-radius component verified at the balanced L-mask scale.",
    "consumer": "The principal frontier theorem uses this certificate for its upper bound.",
    "proof_tex": "On the stable branch, the block identity gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{vec}_{W_n}(L_n)=F_n(\\operatorname{vec}_{\\Omega_n}(L_n)).\n\\]\nLet \\(\\ell_n=\\operatorname{vec}_{\\Omega_n}(L_n)\\) and\n\\(\\widetilde\\ell_n=\\operatorname{vec}_{\\Omega_n}(\\widetilde X_n)\\). From the definition of the center,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{vec}_{W_n}(\\widehat L_n-L_n)\n=\\widehat J_n(X_n^{(2)}-\\ell_n)\n+\\{F_n(\\widetilde\\ell_n)-F_n(\\ell_n)\n-\\widehat J_n(\\widetilde\\ell_n-\\ell_n)\\}.\n\\]\nOn \\(\\mathcal G_n\\cap\\mathcal F_n^c\\), the synchronization envelope bounds every argument perturbation by \\(\\tau_n\\), and stability places every intervening anchor singular value above \\(\\widehat\\delta_n/2\\). Expanding each occurrence of the inverse by\n\\[\n(B+H)^{-1}-B^{-1}\n=-B^{-1}H(B+H)^{-1}\n\\]\non the rank-one row and column spaces, and bounding the three possible placements of two perturbations, gives\n\\[\n\\left\\|\nF_n(\\widetilde\\ell_n)-F_n(\\ell_n)\n-\\widehat J_n(\\widetilde\\ell_n-\\ell_n)\n\\right\\|_*\n\\le\nC_0\\tau_n^2\n\\left(\\widehat\\delta_n^{-1}\n+\\widehat M_n\\widehat\\delta_n^{-2}\n+\\widehat M_n^2\\widehat\\delta_n^{-3}\\right)\n=\\mathscr R_n^{(2)}.\n\\]\nNo division occurs before the stable lower bound \\(\\widehat\\delta_n>0\\) is invoked.\n\nBy the randomized-split lemma, conditionally on \\(X_n^{(1)}\\),\n\\[\n\\widehat J_n(X_n^{(2)}-\\ell_n)\n\\sim\\mathcal N(0,\\widehat\\Sigma_n).\n\\]\nIt therefore has nuclear norm at most \\(\\mathring q_{\\alpha,n}\\) with conditional probability at least \\(1-\\alpha/4\\). Conditional on the pilot, put\n\\[\nB_k=\\mathbf1\\{T_k<\\mathring q_{\\alpha,n}\\}.\n\\]\nBy the definition of the left \\(1-\\alpha/4\\) quantile,\n\\(\\mathbb E(B_k\\mid X_n^{(1)})\\le1-\\alpha/4\\). Applying exponential Markov inequality to\n\\(\\prod_k\\mathbb E[\\exp\\{t(B_k-\\mathbb EB_k)\\}\\mid X_n^{(1)}]\\), and optimizing over \\(t>0\\), gives the Bernoulli bound\n\\[\n\\Pr\\!\\left\\{K_n^{-1}\\sum_{k=1}^{K_n}B_k>\n1-\\alpha/4+\\eta_n\n\\ \\middle|\\ X_n^{(1)}\\right\\}\n\\le e^{-2K_n\\eta_n^2}\n=\\frac{\\alpha}{8n}.\n\\]\nOn its complement, fewer than \\(K_np_n\\) simulated values are strictly below\n\\(\\mathring q_{\\alpha,n}\\), so the definition of \\(p_n\\) ensures\n\\(\\widehat q_{\\alpha,n}\\ge\\mathring q_{\\alpha,n}\\). The balanced Gaussian rectangle bound gives\n\\(\\Pr(\\mathcal G_n^c)<\\alpha/4\\). A union bound, using \\(n\\ge2\\), shows that on the stable branch the nuclear ball fails with probability at most\n\\[\n\\frac{\\alpha}{4}+\\frac{\\alpha}{4}+\\frac{\\alpha}{16}<\\alpha.\n\\]\nOn the fallback branch,\n\\[\n\\|P_{W_n}L_n\\|_*\n\\le\\|L_n\\|_*\n\\le r\\,s_+\\sqrt{N_nT_n}\n\\le s_+\\sqrt{r\\min\\{N_n,T_n\\}N_nT_n},\n\\]\nwhere the last inequality uses \\(r\\le\\min\\{N_n,T_n\\}\\). Since the fallback center is zero, coverage there is deterministic. This proves\n\\((\\widehat L_n,\\widehat R_{\\alpha,n})\\in\\mathcal H_{\\alpha,n}\\).\n\nThe totality lemma gives randomized polynomial time. Finally, the radius audit and the fallback identity \\(N_n=T_n=n\\), \\(r=1\\), give\n\\[\n\\sup_{\\vartheta_n\\in\\Theta_n}\\mathbb E\\widehat R_{\\alpha,n}\n\\le\n\\sup_{\\vartheta_n}\\mathbb E[\n\\widehat q_{\\alpha,n}\\mathbf1_{\\mathcal F_n^c}]\n+\\sup_{\\vartheta_n}\\mathbb E[\n\\mathscr R_n^{(2)}\\mathbf1_{\\mathcal F_n^c}]\n+s_+n^{3/2}\\sup_{\\vartheta_n}\\Pr(\\mathcal F_n)\n\\le C\\sigma\\sqrt n.\n\\]",
